A||||8||MP5Na3||Wow, just got the suspension done on my GT ||||10||
Z||000000||MP5Na3||09-12-2001||06:57 PM||||Just added 17inch rims 215/40R17 front 235/45R17 rear, front and rear koni swaybars and kyb shocks and struts, suspension technology lowering springs and just took it out on the twisties for the first time today.  Jeez that car handles good now.  Honest to goodness it runs better through corners than I am willing to push it (gimme time on that one).  Anyhow, I guess I never realized how good these cars could handle.  My only gripe about the whole thing is the lowering springs.  They were supposed to drop it 1.5 inches.  Instead they dropped the front 3/4 and the rear 1/4.  Eh, I'll live with it.  But anyhow, that was one incredible transition the car went through.  Highly recommend it to anyone who is thinking about doing this to their cars.  I honestly don't think either my vette or TA ever handled this good.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||09-12-2001||10:05 PM||||Hey MP5Na3!<BR>What compound is that?<BR>Oh Ya! Congrats on the suspention!<BR>Give the springs time to settle, and remeasure in a couple of days...<BR>I replaced the ball joints in front along with poly bushings, and I had a 3 inch rise on the front!<BR>Talk about worn out suspention...<BR>Now I need to do the rear!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierochild||09-12-2001||10:46 PM||||MP5Na3, I know what you mean about handling.  I've done most of my 86GT.  Poly on cradle and rear suspension.  Held adjustable coilovers on KYB struts, with 300lbs springs. Front sway bar mounted on rear.  Poly bushings and end links on front sway bar.  Moved battery up front.  Running Sumitomo HTR Z II 215/40R17 on rear, and 205/40R17 up front for street.  225/45R15 Hoosiers all the way for autocross.  It sticks to the track like a formula race car.  Led a pack of six Fieros through the North Georgia twisties this past weekend. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"> Only one could stay with me. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  And that was on street tires.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SLYGUY||09-13-2001||03:40 AM||||I took my brother up into the mountains by our house and he was surprised too. I used to ride a 600cc motorcycle that could seriously carve, and he is a seasoned rider and both of us were amazed at how this car can handle bone stock. Never driven a car in the twisties that refuses to lean, and will turn as sharp as you dare. Heck, you practically need a helmet to protect your head from the window!!<P>Great cars all the way around as far as I'm concerned.<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Joe Torma||09-13-2001||05:05 AM||||If you have the Suspension Techniques springs...don't worry they settle in...they dropped my car 2"<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/amsig.gif"> <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||MP5Na3||09-13-2001||06:06 AM||||Fierowrecker,<P>If by compound you are refering to my handle, I picked it up from my occupation.  I work as a gunsmith and one of my favorite toys is the Heckler & Koch MP5Na3 9mm submachinegun.  In use by various police forces/military throughout the world.  Most people don't realize civilians can own fully automaric weapons.  A lot of FBI and ATF background checks and paperwork, but they are a lot of fun also (but expensive, a civilian transferable MP5 can run close to 10K).<P>Back to topic though, I have yet to do the poly cradle bushings and battery relocation.  Worth doing??  Was gonna do the cradle bushings earlier but the d*mn bolts were so tight I just gave up on it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||09-13-2001||10:36 PM||||Congrats!!  It's amazing how awesome these cars are with 17" wheels and rubber.<P>Enjoy!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierochild||09-14-2001||10:42 PM||||MP5Na3,  As I was building my suspension I took in one step at a time with driving time before the next step. I started with wheels and tires. Then coilovers/struts, rear bushings (cradle and A arms), front sway bar bushings and endlinks, rear swaybar, and then moving the battery.  Out of all these doing the rear bushings made the most dramatic handling change.  My car is an 86 with 115XXX on it.  The old rubber bushuings were really bad due to age and wear.  I used the drill method to get the old ones out, and the polys slid right in.<P>86 GT<BR>Neon Green<BR>pic coming soon ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fierowrecker||09-15-2001||06:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MP5Na3:<BR><B>Fierowrecker,<P>If by compound you are refering to my handle, I picked it up from my occupation.  I work as a gunsmith and one of my favorite toys is the Heckler & Koch MP5Na3 9mm submachinegun.  In use by various police forces/military throughout the world.  Most people don't realize civilians can own fully automaric weapons.  A lot of FBI and ATF background checks and paperwork, but they are a lot of fun also (but expensive, a civilian transferable MP5 can run close to 10K).<P>Back to topic though, I have yet to do the poly cradle bushings and battery relocation.  Worth doing??  Was gonna do the cradle bushings earlier but the d*mn bolts were so tight I just gave up on it.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I can only afford to put my money into my Fiero, but I can dream though!<BR>Almost bought a thompson a few years ago for 250, but didn't pursue it...<BR>It and I are not registered!<BR>Besides, I like the 50 cal. BMG rifes out there!<BR>Yes, the battery relocation is worth it, try two bags of shot in the front to see if you like what the relocation will do...<BR>Have heard, but haven't replaced my cradle mount bushings yet, that it stiffins up the rear nicely!<P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||1
